#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Grade local models on a fixed item set, on this machine.

Published benchmark scores describe a model at full precision on someone
else's hardware. What runs here is a 4- or 8-bit build under MLX or llama.cpp,
and the point of this script is to measure *that* -- the thing you would
actually get if you sent the question to localhost.

Two auto-gradable tasks, sampled once with a fixed seed into
`quality/items.jsonl` (see `quality/make-items.py` for provenance):

  gsm8k   grade-school math, graded on the final number. Exercises reasoning.
  mmlu    4-way multiple choice across 57 subjects, graded on the letter.
          Exercises knowledge recall. Sampled evenly per subject.

And a third, run only when asked for with `--task code`, from
`quality/code-items.jsonl` (see `quality/make-code-items.py`):

  code    LiveCodeBench v6: 175 AtCoder/LeetCode problems. The program is
          run against every hidden test; it scores only if it passes them all.
          Kept out of the composite, so GSM8K+MMLU scores stay comparable
          with runs recorded before it existed.

Two backends, because the two servers speak different dialects:

  lmstudio  POST /api/v0/chat/completions -- returns usage and perf stats.
  fm        Apple's on-device Foundation Model via `fm serve`. Streams SSE
            always, reports no usage, and applies content guardrails that can
            refuse an item outright (recorded as `refused`, scored wrong).

On trusting the server
----------------------
LM Studio resolves an unknown `model@variant` to whichever model is already
loaded, with no error. Every response is checked against the id that was
requested, because otherwise a variant that has been deleted from disk quietly
reports the resident model's numbers under the missing variant's name.

On reasoning models
-------------------
Qwen3.x thinks by default, and LM Studio honours neither `/no_think` nor
`chat_template_kwargs.enable_thinking`. It does honour an assistant prefill of
an empty `<think></think>` block, which is what `--no-think` (the default)
sends. Thinking is left ON with `--think`.

This matters more than it looks. With thinking on, a single multiple-choice
item costs 300+ tokens of reasoning, which on the slowest variant here turns
the 750-item set into a ~10 hour run. It also makes the comparison against
Apple's non-reasoning `fm` model meaningless. Scores from `--think` and
`--no-think` runs are not comparable to each other; the mode is recorded in
the output.

Determinism: temperature 0 throughout. Results are still not bit-reproducible
(Metal kernels reassociate floating point), but run-to-run drift is small.

Resumability: every graded item is appended to the output JSONL as it lands.
Re-running with the same `-o` skips items already present, so a run that dies
four hours in does not start over.

Usage:
    ./quality-bench lmstudio:qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b@4bit -o out.jsonl
    ./quality-bench fm:system -o out.jsonl
    ./quality-bench --task code lmstudio:qwen/qwen3.6-27b@4bit -o code.jsonl
    ./quality-bench --score out.jsonl
    ./quality-bench --regrade code.jsonl     # re-run the grader on stored code
"""

